# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: `core.autocrlf = true` is set on this machine and there was
# no `.gitattributes`, so every checkout rewrote Spire's byte-exact receipts to CRLF.
# Spire's `canonicalJson()` emits LF and its installer compares BYTES, so the
# integrity check could never pass on a Windows clone — measured 2026-08-14 against
# the 348-artifact receipt: 114 matched as-is, 231 matched only after CRLF->LF.
# Git stores these as LF already; the damage was purely checkout-side, which is why
# normalizing the working tree alone did not stick and this file is the real fix.
#
# The same defect class bit `ai-rec-grounding` at GATE Q (finding F-2): a Rust
# source-scan guard matching a literal "\n" failed on any fresh Windows clone.

# Spire kit + product memory: receipts are machine-compared byte-for-byte.
.spire/**           text eol=lf
# The Windows launcher shim is a batch file the installer writes with CRLF, and the
# receipt pins those bytes. The blanket `eol=lf` above overshot onto it, so every
# checkout rewrote it to LF and cluster entry died on "artifact integrity" before a
# single gate could run (found 2026-09-02). Last matching rule wins, so this follows.
.spire/bin/spire.cmd text eol=crlf
# Render evidence is binary and must never pass through line-ending filters.
.spire/**/*.jpeg    binary
.spire/**/*.jpg     binary
.spire/**/*.png     binary

# Host adapters: these carry the installed kit artifacts whose sha256 the
# installation receipt pins.
.claude/**          text eol=lf
.codex/**           text eol=lf
.grok/**            text eol=lf

# Source scanned by structural guards that match on "\n" (see F-2 above).
*.rs                text eol=lf
*.mjs               text eol=lf

# API contract artifacts are raw-byte oracles and must not be rewritten by a
# Windows checkout before the non-writing manifest check compares them.
apps/api/openapi/*.json text eol=lf
apps/api/tests/fixtures/api_contract/*.json text eol=lf
# Same rule for the capacity v1 byte-identity oracle: manifest.json, the seam patch and
# the decision-bearing before/ + inputs/ fixtures are SHA-256 / byte compared by
# apps/api/src/capacity/identity.rs, which never rewrites them.
apps/api/tests/fixtures/capacity-baseline/** text eol=lf
libs/api/src/generated/*.ts text eol=lf

# Browser visual-review receipts and source patches are SHA-256-bound evidence.
apps/dashboard-e2e/visual-review/**/*.json  text eol=lf
apps/dashboard-e2e/visual-review/**/*.patch text eol=lf
apps/dashboard-e2e/visual-review/**/*.png   binary
apps/dashboard-e2e/src/quality/snapshots/**/*.png binary
